          "content": "Page No. 80"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.36,
            0.293,
            0.381
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.39,
            0.849,
            0.467
          ],
          "content": "1. Why do you think the Chinese alternated between the Zong and Heng symbols? If only the Zong symbols were to be used, how would 41 be represented? Could this numeral be interpreted in any other way if there is no significant space between two successive positions?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.472,
            0.848,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "**Ans.** The Chinese alternated between the Zong (vertical) and Hong (Horizontal) rod numerals to get all the powers of 10 required to get all numbers, and to distinguish between adjacent places."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.553,
            0.325,
            0.597
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "41"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.609,
            0.76,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": "Yes, it might be interpreted as 23, 32 or 122. Think of more."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.65,
            0.847,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": "2. Form a base-2 place value system using ‘ukasar’ and ‘urapon’ as the digits. Compare this system with that of the Gumulgal’s."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.706,
            0.846,
            0.767
          ],
          "content": "**Ans.** In Base-2, we need symbols for numbers 0 and 1. Let us assign 0-urapon and 1-ukasar. Now compare this system with Gumulgal's."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.771,
            0.844,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": "| Number | Base-2 system (ur = 0, uk = 1) | Gumulgal System |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | (1) × 2<sup>0</sup> = uk | ur |\n| 2 | (1) × 21 + (0) × 20 = uk ur | uk |\n| 3 | (1) × 21 + (1) × 20 = uk uk | uk-ur |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_43_table_14.jpg"
